  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nUpper ridging continues over the Northern and Central Plains with an \nupper trough over the northeastern US. A shortwave trough is sliding \nacross the Alberta/Saskatchewan border with another shortwave trough \nover British Columbia. At the surface, a low is over Saskatchewan \nwith troughing extending down into the Central Rockies. A cold front \ndrapes down from this low into eastern Wyoming. In our area, the \nairmass remains relatively the same with minimal inhibition, \nsufficient instability, and moist with dewpoints in the 70s and \nPWATs between 1.85-2\". This will result in isolated showers and \nstorms across much of the area. Chances are expected to decrease \naround sunset this evening. Severe weather is not anticipated with \nthis activity. The main impacts will be heavy rainfall and \noccasional lightning. \n\nFor Monday, an upper-level high is progged to expand across the \nCentral Rockies into the Central Plains. Highs will be around 100 \nareawide on Monday afternoon as above normal 850 mb temperatures are \nexpected. Dewpoints will range from the mid 60s in central KS to low \n70s in southeast KS. This will allow much of the area to see heat \nindices up to 105 with values exceeding 105 mainly in southeast KS. \nAs such, a Heat Advisory remains in effect for Monday afternoon into \nthe evening and has been expanded to the rest of our area. As the \nupper shortwaves move southeast into the Great Lakes, a cold front \nwill push south through the Central Plains and into our area by \nTuesday morning. The front is progged to drape across south-central \nKS by Tuesday afternoon. Highs in central KS will likely be in the \nmid to upper 90s with areas south and along the front seeing \ntemperatures near or greater than 100. The warmest heat indices will \nbe in southern KS with values approaching 110. As such, a Extreme \nHeat Watch remains in effect generally for areas south of Highway \n50. \n\nThe cold front will push south of our area by Wednesday morning, \nreturning high temperatures to the low to mid 90s on Wednesday \nafternoon. Guidance suggests northwesterly flow aloft returning on \nWednesday and continuing through the late next week. This may give \nway to better rain chances across the area on both Wednesday and \nThursday night.",
